WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.220
- [Kim] Hi, this is Kim from Khan Academy,

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.940
and today I'm learning
more about Article VII

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.470
of the U.S. Constitution,
which is the provision

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.450
that specified the conditions

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.570
for the Constitution to become law.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:12.500
It reads,

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.940
The Ratification of the
Conventions of nine States,

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.930
shall be sufficient for the Establishment

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.940
of this Constitution between the States

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.790
so ratifying the Same.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:23.340
Although this sounds simple,

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.490
it reminds us that when
the Framers finished

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:29.410
drafting the Constitution
in Philadelphia in 1787,

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.560
it was by no means a done deal.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.150
At least nine states had
to ratify the Constitution

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.670
in order to replace
the existing government

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.680
under the Articles of Confederation.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.950
To learn more about the
ratification process,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:43.840
I sought out the help of two experts.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.800
Mark Graber is the Jacob
A. France Professor

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.610
of Constitutionalism at
the University of Maryland

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.230
Francis King Carey School of Law.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:55.250
Michael Rappaport is the
Darling Foundation Professor

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.010
at the University of
San Diego School of Law.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.230
Professor Graber, can
you tell us a little bit

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:03.090
about the political
context of ratification?

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.970
What was going on at this time as

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:10.090
the Framers tried to
put a new Constitution

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.700
into the fabric of the United States?

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.810
- [Mark] Well, one central problem

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.030
of the Articles of Confederation
was the Articles required

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:23.680
that all 13 states
consent for any amendment.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.280
And it turned out, at this time,

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.250
Rhode Island was a great outlier,

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.930
so Rhode Island wasn't going
to consent to much of anything.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.730
And, in fact, Rhode Island
did not even send delegates

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.490
to the convention that
drafted the Constitution.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.220
So the Framers knew

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.890
that if you had the unanimous
rule for ratification,

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.480
it would not work.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.393
So instead, they chose
nine, it's about 2/3, 3/4,

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.460
in part to make sure
Rhode Island, one outlier,

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.610
could not prevent adoption.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.500
- [Michael] So let's remember,
there's two stages here

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.790
in how the Constitution
gets written and ratified.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.690
So first, it's written

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.080
in what's called the Drafting Convention

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.790
or the Philadelphia Convention,

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:09.930
which was held during the summer of 1787.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:14.030
And in that convention, it
was pretty much in agreement

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:18.040
that the Federal Government
needed to be made stronger,

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.300
but that was just a proposal.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.440
In order for the
Constitution to be ratified,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.550
it needed nine of the 13 states,

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.240
and so it went to the second stage.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.790
And there, things were, in some ways,

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.090
gonna be more difficult

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.390
because there was a variety of viewpoints

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:37.900
in the different states.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.670
And the main question that came up

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.030
in state after state after state was

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.560
was the Federal Government being given

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.070
too much additional power?

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.160
Under the previous regime of
the Articles of Confederation,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.610
the Federal Government
had very limited powers,

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.093
and the Constitution was gonna

00:02:55.093 --> 00:02:57.230
give the Federal Government more power.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:59.640
- [Mark] They wanted a strategy that,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.083
once the ball started rolling,

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:04.600
states that were slower

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.070
were going to be faced with a choice.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:10.100
You could get in on the inside

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.970
and maybe effect some changes early.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.010
But if you were left out,

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:16.890
whatever happened would
happen without you,

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:18.360
and a lot of states, at the end,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.870
were fearful of being left out.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:22.060
- [Michael] This strategy worked.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.130
And as they went through in
tough states, as time went on,

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.520
so New Hampshire, Virginia,

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.240
New York, North Carolina, Rhode Island,

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.940
in order to get ratification,

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.830
each time, they promised,
we'll have a Bill of Rights

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.220
and those states added

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.350
a list of amendments that they wanted

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:46.070
added to the Constitution.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.000
- [Kim] So was it controversial
that the Framers decided

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.680
that only nine states would be necessary

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.790
to ratify the Constitution?

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.260
- [Mark] Very controversial.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.510
One of the central
points of Anti-Federalist

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.780
was that this was illegal,

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.010
that the existing
Constitution said all states,

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:07.420
and therefore only all states
could change the Constitution.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.420
- [Michael] So in that respect,

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.000
it was somewhat controversial.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.876
Another way in which it
departed from the Articles

00:04:12.876 --> 00:04:16.760
is the Articles said you
needed the state legislatures

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:18.900
to approve the amendments,

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:20.780
and the U.S. Constitution said, no,

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:23.210
we want state conventions,

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:26.190
special bodies elected by the people,

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.620
to approve these things.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:28.810
Why did they say that?

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.240
Because they feared that
the state legislatures,

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.610
who would be losing a lot of
power under the Constitution,

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:34.830
would vote against it.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:38.450
So they wanted sort of to
go directly to the people

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:41.630
in these conventions and
bypass the state legislatures.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:43.960
- [Kim] So we know that
there were some opponents

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.970
of this new Constitution.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:47.630
How close did they actually come

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.800
to preventing its ratification?

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.620
- [Michael] Well, it
was a very close fight.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:54.880
We look back on it,

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.940
and eventually all 13
states are gonna ratify,

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.710
and it just looks like, oh well,

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.590
that wasn't too much of a trouble.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:03.300
But it was very close,

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:07.120
and things could have easily
gone in the opposite direction.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.270
One of the ways in which it was close was

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:12.170
that there were just very close votes.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.790
Massachusetts was

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:17.330
187 to 168.

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:20.850
New Hampshire was 57-47.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.490
New York was 30-27.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.780
So very close votes involved,

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:28.470
but, you know, a couple of
people changing their mind,

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:32.330
and that would've meant
various states didn't ratify.

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:34.380
In addition to that,

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.830
some of the states
actually did not ratify.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.200
So the first thing that
happened was Rhode Island,

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.190
early on in the process, says,

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:45.030
we don't like your horrible Constitution.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:46.250
They all expected that.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:47.970
We're not gonna hold even a convention.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:48.960
You want us to hold a convention.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.230
We're not gonna hold a convention.

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:53.290
We're just gonna have a vote in state.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:54.557
And that vote in the state,

00:05:54.557 --> 00:05:57.490
90% of the people voted
against the Constitution.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:01.500
So, in a way, Rhode Island actually

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.110
voted against ratification,

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:05.850
although it didn't use the proper method.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:09.390
North Carolina, also,
they held a convention,

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.460
and they were very upset
about there not being

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.980
a Bill of Rights in the Constitution.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:15.780
And they just didn't approve it.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:17.050
They didn't disapprove it,

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:19.750
they just did nothing, and they waited.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:22.840
So, in a way, two of the states

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:24.510
voted against ratification.

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:27.840
- [Mark] North Carolina
had not yet ratified

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:29.610
when George Washington took office.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.440
So, in fact, when George
Washington took office,

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.120
there were only 11 states in the Union.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.960
At the end of the day, crucial people,

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:42.610
I think, decided it was better
to sign the Constitution

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:45.510
and be in on the ground floor

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:48.070
than stay out and see what happened.

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:50.610
- [Kim] Interesting, so who
are some of the major players

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:53.520
involved here, and what
were they arguing about?

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.138
- [Michael] There were the Federalists,

00:06:55.138 --> 00:06:59.815
who were arguing in favor of
ratifying the Constitution,

00:06:59.815 --> 00:07:01.157
and the Anti-Federalists,

00:07:01.157 --> 00:07:04.610
who were arguing against
ratifying the Constitution.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:06.180
So the Federalists,

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:09.810
two of the very famous
ones are familiar names,

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:13.180
Alexander Hamilton and James Madison.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:16.220
Those two people, though, were
also particularly important

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:18.267
because they decided, Hamilton said,

00:07:18.267 --> 00:07:19.427
"We're gonna have a tough time

00:07:19.427 --> 00:07:21.527
"getting ratification in New York.

00:07:21.527 --> 00:07:25.320
"We need to write some essays
defending the Constitution."

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:28.790
And so Hamilton and
Madison cooperated together

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:31.350
and wrote what we now call
The Federalist Papers,

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:36.020
which were simply essays
written in the newspapers,

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:39.540
trying to urge the New York Convention

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:41.410
to ratify the Constitution.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:44.710
Eventually, those essays
were sent to other states

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:49.630
and became known, and now we
come to revere those essays

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:51.960
as The Federalist Papers,
but they were originally

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.890
just kind of advocacy pieces
for ratifying the convention.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:59.340
Now, there were also Anti-Federalists.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.300
- [Mark] The Anti-Federalists, in part,

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:04.440
were simply people who
opposed the Constitution.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:07.700
And just as is the case that,
say, people opposed Obamacare

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.070
from both the left and the right,

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:12.370
people who opposed the Constitution

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:14.300
opposed it for many different reasons,

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:18.370
and one of their problems was
they were not a united bunch.

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:21.090
But, in general, these were people fearful

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:24.060
of a very strong national government.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:26.310
They believed states were sovereign,

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.730
they wanted to keep power local,

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:30.290
and they were very fearful of

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:32.370
what they perceived to be an elite,

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:36.660
who would run the Constitution
after ratification.

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:40.240
The names that people may
know who were Anti-Federalists

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.570
were people like Patrick
Henry and Samuel Adams.

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:47.350
- [Kim] So what were some
of the major arguments made

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:50.360
in favor of adopting
this new Constitution?

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:53.980
- [Michael] There were, I
think, two types of arguments.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:58.020
People felt that the Articles
were not working properly.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:00.440
They had all kinds of problems.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:02.620
And one of the strongest arguments

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:05.200
that The Federalist Papers made was that

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:10.200
if we don't fix this and make
for a stronger union together,

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:12.720
what'll happen is, we'll break apart.

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:16.490
Look at what happens when
you have a bunch of states

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:19.380
all next to one another in a land area.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.910
We know what that looks
like, it's called Europe.

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:23.210
And what happens in Europe?

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.980
They fight wars with one
another all the time,

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:28.890
and those wars are very problematic.

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:32.710
You have to have big
armies, standing armies.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:35.000
Now, there were also the
particular arguments about

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.890
what powers were missing that
the Federal Government had.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:40.340
The main arguments were made was

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:42.570
the Federal Government
did not have enough power.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:45.740
So they didn't have, for
example, the taxing power,

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:49.540
and they didn't have a
way of enforcing treaties

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:51.790
against the states very well.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.180
And some of the states
were putting tariffs up,

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.670
so interfering with
trade within the country.

00:09:56.670 --> 00:10:00.400
- [Mark] So states would set
tariffs on out-of-state goods.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:03.900
States wouldn't contribute
to the national government.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:07.070
Second, we needed to
present a united front

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:09.110
to foreign governments.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.510
So we were very worried, what would happen

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:15.300
if South Carolina formed
an alliance with England

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.170
and North Carolina formed
an alliance with France?

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:19.140
That wouldn't be good.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:22.190
The country needed to
speak with one voice.

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:23.880
- [Kim] So those are some
of the arguments made

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.790
for adopting the Constitution.

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:27.610
What were some of the arguments against

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:30.270
adopting this new Constitution?

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:32.320
- [Mark] Well, first argument was,

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:34.730
even though most Anti-Federalists admitted

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:37.150
the Articles needed some repair,

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:39.030
they said it's not really urgent.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.820
It's not like the house is
gonna fall down tomorrow.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.180
It's just, you know, the
wind is coming through,

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:45.870
and we can figure it out.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:48.880
We really want a better Constitution.

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:51.420
The other arguments were the Constitution

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.900
put too much power in
the national government.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:56.900
It put too much power in elites.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:00.030
The fear was if you
had national elections,

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:01.960
only elites would win.

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:03.560
If you had local elections,

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:07.060
people the people actually knew would win.

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:10.580
- [Kim] So they thought
a big national government

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:13.450
would be too far away from the people

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:15.440
to know what they really needed,

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:17.210
as opposed to state governments,

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:20.310
which they perceived as
being sort of more personal,

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:23.480
closer to the needs of individuals?

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:26.470
- [Mark] So compare
two kinds of elections.

00:11:26.470 --> 00:11:30.680
First, how many people really know anyone

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:34.480
who runs for senator,
who runs for president,

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:37.580
that you're on a first-name basis with?

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:40.420
Now compare, you're in a school club.

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:43.120
Chances are, when someone
runs for an officer

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:45.310
of the school club, you know who they are.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:47.150
- [Michael] Another type of argument was,

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:50.360
if you give the Federal
Government this power,

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:52.550
and even if, and the
Federalists will always argue

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:55.200
that the power was limited
that they were being given

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:56.540
to the Federal Government.

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.200
And the Anti-Federalist
came back and says,

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.540
well, you say it's limited,

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.470
but what we know from
historical experience is,

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:06.010
is that once a government's in power,

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:08.700
it tends to seize more power.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:12.540
There's a lot of vague
phrases in the Constitution,

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:16.440
and the Federal Government
will use those vague phrases

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:18.720
to assert greater and greater power.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.300
- [Kim] What were some of the strategies

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:24.050
that the Framers used to
entice some of the opponents

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:27.180
of a strong central government
to ratify the Constitution?

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:29.640
- [Michael] So the first
thing that they did was

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:32.010
to try to build up momentum.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:34.400
So they knew there were certain states

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:37.200
which were strongly in
favor of ratification.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.760
Small states sometimes
wanted ratification.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:44.010
So the first state that
comes in is Delaware,

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:46.850
and they vote 30 to zero
for the Constitution.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:49.030
And a bunch of early states,

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:53.140
so let's say the first five states,

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:57.090
all vote for ratification
by pretty lopsided margins.

00:12:57.090 --> 00:12:59.300
So that builds up a kind of momentum.

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:02.230
All right, we have five
states, we only need four more.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.820
Of course, the next ones
that were gonna come

00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:06.520
were gonna be much more difficult.

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:11.800
And they then needed to change

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:13.200
their strategy at that point.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:15.290
- [Mark] You know, they argued like crazy.

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:18.890
The Federalist Papers are
a very famous example.

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:21.670
They indicated they'd be open to amendment

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:24.803
once the Constitution was ratified.

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:27.820
Then, again, in Pennsylvania,

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:30.680
when Anti-Federalists
boycotted the convention,

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.520
and the result is the
convention didn't have a quorum.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:36.730
They ordered the Sergeant
at Arms to a tavern.

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:39.790
The Sergeant at Arms found some
boycotting Anti-Federalists

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:41.420
and physically put them in the building

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:43.313
so they could count for a quorum.

00:13:43.313 --> 00:13:44.990
- [Kim] (laughing) That's terrific.

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:46.540
- [Mark] That one doesn't
get told in a whole lot

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:48.910
of history books.
(Kim laughs)

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:49.743
- [Kim] Wow.

00:13:49.743 --> 00:13:51.810
- [Mark] The Framers could
play rough-and-tumble politics

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:53.060
with the best of them.

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:54.850
- [Kim] A lot of the things that pop up

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:56.700
at the time of the framing,

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:59.560
I think, we look back
on today and imagine,

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:02.650
how would this work in today's era,

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:06.590
with, you know, 330 million Americans?

00:14:06.590 --> 00:14:10.440
Do you think the system under
the Articles of Confederation

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:13.200
with a smaller, more local government

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:14.633
could possibly work today?

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:16.690
- [Mark] Well, among other things,

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:18.480
the Articles had no great means

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.140
of collecting taxes or gaining revenue.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:24.690
Given that modern government
needs trillions of dollars,

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:26.770
the Articles, if you
got a thousand dollars,

00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:30.280
it was a miracle, so the
Articles clearly don't work.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:33.240
But the Articles were inadequate

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:36.010
for 18th-century government.

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:38.180
They were clearly inadequate

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:41.260
or would be clearly inadequate
for 21st-century government.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:44.240
Now, the Constitution
appears to have been adequate

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:45.960
for 18th-century government.

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:48.450
Whether it is adequate for
21st-century government

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:49.930
is a fair question.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:52.240
- [Michael] One of the
interesting questions about

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:56.820
the Constitution is how
democratic is the Constitution?

00:14:56.820 --> 00:15:00.300
This debate about how
democratic the Constitution is

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:04.170
actually enters into the
question about ratification.

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:06.020
On one level,

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:09.100
the Constitution was democratic

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:12.930
because each state in these
ratification conventions

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:17.710
voted on whether to
ratify by majority vote.

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:20.050
On the other hand, at the federal level,

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:24.060
the ratification requirement
required nine out of 13.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:28.340
And the idea there would
be we needed more buy-in.

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:31.450
We needed a limitation
on simple majority rule

00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:34.460
in order to make the system function well,

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:37.660
in order to produce a Constitution

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:40.830
that would have support
from the whole country.

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:43.630
The interesting thing about
that supermajority rule

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:45.340
and something that people don't often

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:48.380
make the connection
with is we probably owe

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:52.280
our Bill of Rights to
that supermajority rule.

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:56.533
Because if only seven of the
13 states, the mere majority,

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:00.860
had been needed to
ratify the Constitution,

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:04.670
then it's quite possible
that the Federalists

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:08.160
wouldn't have had to promise
to put a Bill of Rights

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:11.190
into the Constitution because
they wouldn't have needed to.

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:13.810
And they were initially quite opposed

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:16.260
to putting a Bill of
Rights in the Constitution.

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:18.330
It was only the fact that they needed nine

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:22.720
out of the 13 states that
really forced their hand

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:24.180
and forced them to promise

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:25.870
that they would put the
Bill of Rights in there.

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:28.760
So we see the, in terms
of the ratification,

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:30.790
that there's both sort
of democratic elements

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:35.640
but also republican or
supermajority elements.

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.470
- [Kim] So we've learned
that it wasn't easy

00:16:37.470 --> 00:16:39.200
to ratify the Constitution.

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:41.570
The Framers bypassed state legislatures

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:44.820
and went directly to the
people in state conventions,

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:46.900
hoping that momentum and arguments

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:49.610
for a stronger Federal
Government would entice

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:52.510
the opponents of the
Constitution to ratify it.

00:16:52.510 --> 00:16:55.250
It took until 1789

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:59.690
for nine out of 13 states
to ratify the Constitution

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:01.490
and finally make it law.

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:03.890
To learn more about Article VII, check out

00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:07.410
the National Constitution
Center's Interactive Constitution

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:08.970
and Khan Academy's resources

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:10.993
on U.S. government and politics.

